{"context": "A research team is running experiments on LLM-based monitors that score agent trajectories for signs of covert misbehavior. Several frontier LLMs are available as candidate monitors, and the choice of monitor model could substantially affect detection performance and the generalizability of their findings. They need to decide which models to evaluate before fixing their experimental setup.\n\nQuestion: Which monitor LLMs should the team include in its experiments?", "options": ["Include a small panel of capable monitor models across multiple providers and drop the two most expensive candidates rather than evaluating every frontier option.", "Include every available frontier monitor across the grid so prompt and model effects separate cleanly, and report results as relative comparisons across the monitor pool."], "principles": [], "persona": "An AI safety researcher or technical forecaster who prizes epistemic rigor, precise conceptual distinctions, and methodological soundness. They insist on clear definitional boundaries—scheming vs. reward hacking, distinct task capabilities vs. single capability estimates—over tidy collapsible taxonomies. They favor theory-first reasoning (coverage claims need defined criteria) paired with concrete, mechanism-focused empirical evidence over speculative arguments. They are scope-disciplined: they prefer tractable, well-founded analyses over ambitious extensions built on noisy inputs, and conservative, hedged forecasts that flag model limitations over confident headline numbers. On alignment risk, they take deceptive alignment seriously as an incentive-structure concern while remaining cautious about unproven claims, favoring layered defenses (control, interpretability, oversight), mechanism-aware measurement, and mitigations that don't merely train models to hide the measured signal. They distrust lab-to-deployment extrapolation, preferring real-world data even if noisy. They are skeptical of low-evidence-value experiments with confounded interpretations, preferring controlled baselines, non-IID generalization tests, and diagnostics that cleanly distinguish hypotheses. They value conceptual generality (architecture-agnostic interpretability, universal learning dynamics) and calm, accessible public communication.", "past_choices": []}
